%S A401741 37,229,409,1261,1789,2041,4069,4381,7969,11041,14089,12181,17341,
%T A401741 14449,28009,29341,35869,38509,36181,53209,44269,65701,76129,83161,
%U A401741 74209,90301,105709,115921,105229,105769,123589,163969,175621,185809,208429,215581,231709
%N A401741 Sum of the squares of the semiperimeter s and inradius r of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse A002144(n): a(n) = s^2 + r^2.
%C A401741 For p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, the odd leg is u = x^2 - y^2 and the even leg is v = 2*x*y. The semiperimeter is s = (u+v+p)/2 = x*(x+y) and the inradius is r = (u+v-p)/2 = y*(x-y). Either leg may be the longer one.
%C A401741 Equivalently, a(n) is the squared radius of the triangle's Conway circle, whose center is the incenter. A088544 gives the same quantity for all primitive Pythagorean triangles ordered by the product of their three side lengths (A063011). Here only prime hypotenuses are used, in increasing order.
%C A401741 Terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of a(n); for example, a(11) = 14089 > a(12) = 12181.
%C A401741 A401740 lists the Pythagorean primes p for which a(n) is prime. Every prime factor of a(n) is congruent to 1 modulo 12 (A068228); see the linked note.
%C A401741 For p = A002144(n), let Q = p*a(n). The construction in A401742 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct positive integer entries, at least six of them squares. This works whether a(n) is prime or composite.
%H A401741 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401741/b401741.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A401741 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A401741 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A401741 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A401741 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A401741 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/a401742.txt">Semiperimeter and inradius of Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401741 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401741 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401741 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401741 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ConwayCircle.html">Conway Circle</a>.
%F A401741 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are its odd and even legs, then a(n) = ((u+v+p)/2)^2 + ((u+v-p)/2)^2 = u^2 + u*v + v^2 = p^2 + u*v.
%F A401741 If p = A002144(n) = x^2 + y^2 with integers x > y > 0, then a(n) = x^2*(x+y)^2 + y^2*(x-y)^2 = p^2 + 2*x*y*(x^2-y^2).
%F A401741 a(n) = A002144(n)^2 + A002365(n)*A002366(n).
%F A401741 a(n) = A088544(j(n)), where j(n) is the unique index j for which A088511(j) = A002144(n).
%e A401741 The first Pythagorean prime is 5, whose primitive triangle has legs 3 and 4, semiperimeter 6 and inradius 1. Thus a(1) = 6^2 + 1^2 = 37.
%e A401741 The next two Pythagorean primes are 13 and 17, with odd and even legs (5, 12) and (15, 8), respectively. Consequently, a(2) = 15^2 + 2^2 = 229 and a(3) = 20^2 + 3^2 = 409.
%e A401741 For p = 29, a(4) = 35^2 + 6^2 = 1261 = 13*97.
%o A401741 (Python)
%o A401741 from math import isqrt
%o A401741 from sympy import primerange
%o A401741 def a401741(limit=500):
%o A401741     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A401741     out = []
%o A401741     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A401741         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A401741             continue
%o A401741         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A401741             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A401741             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A401741                 s, r = x*(x+y), y*(x-y)
%o A401741                 q = s*s + r*r
%o A401741                 out.append(q)
%o A401741                 break
%o A401741     return out
%Y A401741 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A063011, A068228, A088511, A088544, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401742.
